1
00:01:09,634 --> 00:01:22,062
Moorgate Passage.

2
00:01:24,691 --> 00:01:25,774
I see it over there!

3
00:01:32,115 --> 00:01:35,284
Bleedin' ratbags.
What's gotten into them?

4
00:01:43,376 --> 00:01:46,336
What in God's name is that?

5
00:01:50,467 --> 00:01:57,056
Halt!

6
00:01:58,808 --> 00:02:02,394
-Blimey!
-Stop!

7
00:02:02,395 --> 00:02:03,771
-Come on!
-Halt!

8
00:02:03,772 --> 00:02:05,439
In the name of the law!

9
00:02:07,525 --> 00:02:10,944
-Halt!
-How the hell do we stop this thing?

10
00:02:10,945 --> 00:02:13,572
I said, halt!

11
00:02:57,992 --> 00:02:59,201
Here.

12
00:02:59,202 --> 00:03:01,870
Careful, mates. Careful.

13
00:03:07,502 --> 00:03:08,877
There's men inside!

14
00:03:30,441 --> 00:03:31,942
Please.

15
00:04:02,765 --> 00:04:05,434
Bring the scientist with us.
Kill any who resist.

16
00:04:07,812 --> 00:04:10,189
Do we have him?

17
00:04:10,190 --> 00:04:12,691
Draper at your service, sir.

18
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:18,906
The world, Herr Draper.
I want the world.

19
00:04:25,455 --> 00:04:28,332
Really?
That's so frightening.

20
00:05:50,415 --> 00:05:53,417
Don't wander off.

21
00:06:09,726 --> 00:06:12,394
Where's Quatermain?

22
00:06:13,521 --> 00:06:16,273
That gentleman at the back.

23
00:06:27,577 --> 00:06:31,705
Do I have the pleasure of addressing
Allan Quatermain?

24
00:06:32,749 --> 00:06:36,877
Yes, you do, sir. Indeed you do.

25
00:06:36,878 --> 00:06:39,838
You're not what I expected.

26
00:06:39,839 --> 00:06:42,132
I presume you're another traveler...

27
00:06:42,133 --> 00:06:45,344
...got it in your head
to sample the Dark Continent.

28
00:06:45,345 --> 00:06:49,014
And while you're at it,
hunt down old Allan Quatermain...

29
00:06:49,015 --> 00:06:51,475
...and have him tell his adventures.

30
00:06:52,477 --> 00:06:55,437
-Well-
-You just fill a seat, sir.

31
00:06:55,438 --> 00:06:59,066
You can fill my glass.
Bruce, double.

32
00:07:03,071 --> 00:07:07,574
I'll regale you with how I found King
Solomon's Mines, or I could relate my-

33
00:07:07,575 --> 00:07:11,036
It is not your past that interests me.

34
00:07:11,037 --> 00:07:13,330
My name is Sanderson Reed.

35
00:07:13,331 --> 00:07:17,167
I am a representative
of Her Majesty's British government.

36
00:07:17,168 --> 00:07:18,752
The Empire needs you.

37
00:07:19,670 --> 00:07:21,838
But the question is...

38
00:07:21,839 --> 00:07:23,965
...do I need the Empire?

39
00:07:25,051 --> 00:07:27,636
Perhaps I should toddle off.
Should I, Allan?

40
00:07:27,637 --> 00:07:30,430
Yes, of course, Nigel, you toddle off.

41
00:07:30,431 --> 00:07:31,973
Toddling.

42
00:07:31,974 --> 00:07:37,437
Nigel is useful for keeping
the story seekers at bay.

43
00:07:37,438 --> 00:07:39,606
I'm Quatermain.

44
00:07:39,607 --> 00:07:42,442
The Empire is in peril.

45
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:49,074
You're probably too young to know,
but the Empire is always in some peril.

46
00:07:49,075 --> 00:07:53,161
We need you to lead a team
of unique men like yourself...

47
00:07:53,162 --> 00:07:56,081
...to combat this threat.

48
00:07:58,292 --> 00:08:00,293
Regale me.

49
00:08:00,294 --> 00:08:05,507
There is great unrest, countries set at
each other's throats, baying for blood.

50
00:08:05,508 --> 00:08:10,137
The trouble of which I speak could
set a match to the whole thing. War.

51
00:08:10,138 --> 00:08:13,682
-With whom, exactly?
-Everyone.

52
00:08:13,683 --> 00:08:15,600
A world war.

53
00:08:16,477 --> 00:08:21,356
-That notion makes you sweat?
-Heavens, man, doesn't it you?

54
00:08:21,357 --> 00:08:23,817
This is Africa, dear boy.

55
00:08:23,818 --> 00:08:27,112
Sweating is what we do.

56
00:08:27,113 --> 00:08:29,698
Where is your sense of patriotism?

57
00:08:30,658 --> 00:08:33,285
God save the queen.

58
00:08:33,286 --> 00:08:36,663
-God save the queen. God bless her.
-God save the queen.

59
00:08:36,664 --> 00:08:39,916
That's about as patriotic
as it gets around here.

60
00:08:39,917 --> 00:08:42,169
But you're Allan Quatermain.

61
00:08:42,170 --> 00:08:46,590
Stories of your exploits have thrilled
English boys for decades.

62
00:08:46,591 --> 00:08:53,054
That I know, and Nigel has done
a grand job reminding me.

63
00:08:53,055 --> 00:08:57,851
But with each past exploit...

64
00:08:57,852 --> 00:09:01,813
... I've lost friends.
White men and black...

65
00:09:01,814 --> 00:09:04,733
...and much more.

66
00:09:04,734 --> 00:09:07,944
And I am not the man I once was.

67
00:09:08,988 --> 00:09:11,198
The gentleman over there.

68
00:09:13,117 --> 00:09:14,826
-We're in for the long haul.
-Exactly.

69
00:09:14,827 --> 00:09:16,953
-Mr. Quatermain?
-Why, yes indeed, sir.

70
00:09:23,127 --> 00:09:24,961
Wrong Quatermain.

71
00:09:24,962 --> 00:09:26,171
That's him.

72
00:09:29,383 --> 00:09:31,468
Get down!

73
00:09:31,469 --> 00:09:35,305
-They're indestructible.
-No, just armor-plated.

74
00:09:48,319 --> 00:09:51,821
Automatic rifles? Who in God's name
has automatic rifles?

75
00:09:51,822 --> 00:09:54,991
That's unsporting. Probably Belgium.

76
00:10:18,516 --> 00:10:19,808
Allan!

77
00:10:34,949 --> 00:10:36,616
Wicked waste.

78
00:10:38,077 --> 00:10:39,494
Look out!

79
00:11:04,353 --> 00:11:07,606
Rule, Britannia.

80
00:11:07,607 --> 00:11:10,025
Wasn't there another one
of these buggers?

81
00:11:10,026 --> 00:11:11,067
Mr. Quatermain!

82
00:11:13,487 --> 00:11:15,405
Bruce, Matilda.

83
00:11:17,783 --> 00:11:21,202
Our friend might have some information.

84
00:11:23,581 --> 00:11:26,833
But he's so far away.

85
00:11:28,085 --> 00:11:30,253
Yes, I thought he was.

86
00:11:31,505 --> 00:11:34,674
God, I hate getting old.

87
00:11:45,853 --> 00:11:49,147
-Did you mean to just wound him?
-Obviously.

88
00:11:52,443 --> 00:11:56,154
Stop him! I need information!

89
00:12:01,827 --> 00:12:03,953
Bloody poison.

90
00:12:06,123 --> 00:12:10,752
You may have no love for the Empire,
but I know you love Africa.

91
00:12:10,753 --> 00:12:13,755
A war in Europe will spread
to its colonies.

92
00:12:24,517 --> 00:12:27,811
It appears the war has arrived.

93
00:12:28,813 --> 00:12:31,439
Very well, Reed...

94
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:33,566
... I'm in.

95
00:12:34,527 --> 00:12:36,569
Excellent.

96
00:12:36,570 --> 00:12:39,489
Pack for an English summer.

97
00:13:42,887 --> 00:13:44,846
You made good time getting here.

98
00:13:44,847 --> 00:13:49,851
Not as good as Phileas Fogg.
Around the world in 80 days.

99
00:14:12,792 --> 00:14:15,293
Where are we going, Australia?

100
00:14:23,594 --> 00:14:26,513
And here we are, sir.

101
00:14:46,450 --> 00:14:48,660
I don't like theatrics.

102
00:14:48,661 --> 00:14:54,582
After Africa's veldts, London's weather
isn't helping your mood, I see.

103
00:14:54,583 --> 00:14:56,960
Identify yourself.

104
00:14:56,961 --> 00:15:00,004
I'm known by many names,
Mr. Quatermain.

105
00:15:00,005 --> 00:15:04,592
My underlings call me sir.
My superiors call me M.

106
00:15:04,593 --> 00:15:06,469
M?

107
00:15:06,470 --> 00:15:08,513
Just M.

108
00:15:09,473 --> 00:15:14,018
I must say, the delight is mine,
meeting so notable a recruit...

109
00:15:14,019 --> 00:15:18,356
...to this newest generation of the
League of Extraordinary Gentlemen.

110
00:15:18,357 --> 00:15:19,774
League of what?

111
00:15:19,775 --> 00:15:22,777
There have been times
when a danger upon the world...

112
00:15:22,778 --> 00:15:26,739
... required the services
of singular individuals.

113
00:15:26,740 --> 00:15:28,741
How very curious.

114
00:15:30,744 --> 00:15:32,912
This museum is full of the curious.

115
00:15:32,913 --> 00:15:35,832
Allan Quatermain, Captain Nemo.

116
00:15:35,833 --> 00:15:38,960
I know of Mr. Quatermain.

117
00:15:38,961 --> 00:15:41,546
And I know of you, captain.

118
00:15:41,547 --> 00:15:44,424
Rumor has it that you're a pirate.

119
00:15:44,425 --> 00:15:47,468
I'd prefer a less provocative title.

120
00:15:47,469 --> 00:15:50,388
-I'm sure you would.
-Gentlemen, please.

121
00:15:50,389 --> 00:15:52,807
Nations are striking at nations.

122
00:15:52,808 --> 00:15:56,728
Every attack marked by the use
of highly advanced weaponry.

123
00:15:56,729 --> 00:15:59,480
These attacks
are all the work of one man...

124
00:15:59,481 --> 00:16:04,402
-...who calls himself the Fantom.
-Very operatic. What's in it for him?

125
00:16:04,403 --> 00:16:05,862
Profit.

126
00:16:05,863 --> 00:16:10,992
Those machines are his creations, the
work of scientists he holds imprisoned.

127
00:16:10,993 --> 00:16:14,829
His attacks have nations clamoring
for the weapons that assail them.

128
00:16:14,830 --> 00:16:18,833
-Then it's a race for arms.
-And millions will perish.

129
00:16:20,836 --> 00:16:24,339
There's one last chance to avert war.

130
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:27,842
The leaders of Europe
are to meet secretly in Venice.

131
00:16:27,843 --> 00:16:32,388
And you believe that this Fantom
will attack that conference?

132
00:16:32,389 --> 00:16:33,765
If he can find it.

133
00:16:33,766 --> 00:16:38,353
We need a team to stop him in Venice.
This team consists of six members.

134
00:16:38,354 --> 00:16:42,649
-You'll have four days.
-Four days to get to Venice?

135
00:16:42,650 --> 00:16:45,276
-It's impossible.
-Let me worry about that.

136
00:16:47,655 --> 00:16:51,240
Well now,
extraordinary gentlemen indeed.

137
00:16:51,241 --> 00:16:52,951
One of them is late.

138
00:16:53,911 --> 00:16:57,372
-Harker, the chemist.
-Oh, chemist, eh?

139
00:16:57,790 --> 00:17:00,208
Do we get to blow something up then?

140
00:17:01,877 --> 00:17:04,629
My eyesight must be worse
than I thought.

141
00:17:04,630 --> 00:17:07,006
No, your eyesight's fine.

142
00:17:10,594 --> 00:17:13,304
No games, M.

143
00:17:13,305 --> 00:17:17,308
Some time ago, a talented,
albeit misguided, man of science...

144
00:17:17,309 --> 00:17:19,852
...discovered the means
to become invisible.

145
00:17:19,853 --> 00:17:24,941
-Yes, I recall the tale, but didn't he die?
-Well, he did, but his process didn't.

146
00:17:24,942 --> 00:17:28,861
You see, I stole it,
and here I stand for all to see.

147
00:17:28,862 --> 00:17:31,864
-Is this some parlor game?
-Believe it.

148
00:17:33,283 --> 00:17:34,784
Easy now, Allan.

149
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:39,205
I'm feeling a bit of a draft
in my nether regions.

150
00:17:39,206 --> 00:17:43,167
And I must say, it's quite refreshing.

151
00:17:43,168 --> 00:17:50,508
Allow me to introduce myself.
Rodney Skinner, gentleman thief.

152
00:17:50,509 --> 00:17:55,555
Now, I thought invisibility
would be a boon to my work.

153
00:17:55,556 --> 00:18:00,101
Well, you can imagine,
it was my undoing.

154
00:18:00,102 --> 00:18:04,564
Once you're invisible,
it's bloody hard to turn back.

155
00:18:04,565 --> 00:18:06,566
We finally caught him.

156
00:18:06,567 --> 00:18:10,695
And they'll provide an antidote.
Well, that's if I'm a good boy.

157
00:18:10,696 --> 00:18:12,280
And are you a good boy?

158
00:18:13,240 --> 00:18:17,326
I guess you'll find out, won't you?

159
00:18:21,582 --> 00:18:23,416
Am I late?

160
00:18:23,417 --> 00:18:25,877
A woman's prerogative, Mrs. Harker.

161
00:18:25,878 --> 00:18:30,256
Please tell me this is Harker's wife
with a sick note.

162
00:18:30,257 --> 00:18:35,219
Sick would be a mild understatement.
My husband's been dead for years.

163
00:18:35,220 --> 00:18:38,723
Gentlemen, Mrs. Wilhelmina Harker.

164
00:18:38,724 --> 00:18:43,019
Mina's prior acquaintance
with a reluctant League member...

165
00:18:43,020 --> 00:18:46,481
-... may prove useful.
-I'm waiting to be impressed.

166
00:18:46,482 --> 00:18:50,818
The fate of the world is at stake.
There are two more members to recruit.

167
00:18:50,819 --> 00:18:54,572
-The clock hands turn, gentlemen.
-Kicking us out already?

168
00:18:54,573 --> 00:18:57,700
A moment ago,
it was sherry and giggles.

169
00:19:09,421 --> 00:19:14,842
-What in God's name is that?
-I call it an automobile.

170
00:19:14,843 --> 00:19:17,553
Yeah, but what is it?

171
00:19:17,554 --> 00:19:20,598
The future, gentlemen.

172
00:19:20,599 --> 00:19:23,017
The future.

173
00:19:24,061 --> 00:19:28,189
-This is my first mate.
-Call me Ishmael. Please.

174
00:19:56,718 --> 00:20:00,972
So how did M get you?

175
00:20:00,973 --> 00:20:02,473
None of your business.

176
00:20:04,560 --> 00:20:07,395
You're a little testy, Mr. Q.

177
00:20:07,396 --> 00:20:12,775
Mrs. Harker, I doubt if you measure
danger the way I do.

178
00:20:12,776 --> 00:20:16,571
And I imagine you with quite the library,
Mr. Quatermain.

179
00:20:16,572 --> 00:20:20,491
All those books you must have read
merely by looking at their covers.

180
00:20:20,492 --> 00:20:25,872
I've had women along on past exploits
and found them to be, at best...

181
00:20:25,873 --> 00:20:29,333
-...a distraction.
-Do I distract you?

182
00:20:29,334 --> 00:20:33,963
My dear girl, I've buried two wives
and many lovers...

183
00:20:33,964 --> 00:20:37,925
...and I'm in no mood
for more of either.

184
00:20:37,926 --> 00:20:42,138
-You can send them my way-
-Skinner, shut up.

185
00:21:24,473 --> 00:21:28,476
-Shall I wait, captain?
-No, bring my lady to me.

186
00:21:30,896 --> 00:21:33,731
This is a charming spot.

187
00:21:33,732 --> 00:21:36,400
Does Jack the Ripper live here?

188
00:21:47,454 --> 00:21:50,706
-Good evening.
-Mr. Dorian Gray?

189
00:21:50,707 --> 00:21:54,335
-I am indeed.
-We came by way of M.

190
00:21:56,505 --> 00:21:59,257
M for mystery.

191
00:21:59,258 --> 00:22:03,302
Well, I've told him and I'm telling you,
I'm not interested.

192
00:22:03,303 --> 00:22:04,971
Dorian.

193
00:22:09,393 --> 00:22:11,143
Mina?

194
00:22:20,570 --> 00:22:23,447
Charming dГ©cor.

195
00:22:23,448 --> 00:22:25,825
You're missing a picture, Mr. Gray.

196
00:22:25,826 --> 00:22:28,327
And you don't miss a thing,
do you?

197
00:22:28,328 --> 00:22:30,579
Oh, sometimes.

198
00:22:35,794 --> 00:22:37,837
Scotch, anyone?

199
00:22:37,838 --> 00:22:39,588
Please, help yourself.

200
00:22:39,589 --> 00:22:42,425
Don't let it ruin your makeup.

201
00:22:43,760 --> 00:22:48,848
I'm impressed, Mr. Gray. You take
Skinner's uniqueness in your stride.

202
00:22:48,849 --> 00:22:53,853
Yes, well, I've seen too much
in my life to shock easily.

203
00:22:53,854 --> 00:22:58,024
Although, I must say
I was surprised to see you again.

204
00:22:58,025 --> 00:23:00,776
Well, our last parting
was such sweet sorrow.

205
00:23:02,154 --> 00:23:05,614
So you're nothing more
than an enticement.

206
00:23:05,615 --> 00:23:09,327
Nevertheless,
your presence intrigues me.

207
00:23:11,330 --> 00:23:14,165
They say you're indestructible,
Quatermain.

208
00:23:14,166 --> 00:23:18,210
Well, a witch doctor
did bless me once.

209
00:23:18,211 --> 00:23:21,172
I had saved his village.

210
00:23:21,173 --> 00:23:26,218
He said Africa
would never allow me to die.

211
00:23:26,219 --> 00:23:29,347
But you're not in Africa now.

212
00:23:29,348 --> 00:23:30,639
No.

213
00:23:30,640 --> 00:23:35,478
I confess a curiosity as to what
the files say about Mr. Gray.

214
00:23:35,479 --> 00:23:39,148
We, all of us, have traits useful
in this endeavor.

215
00:23:39,149 --> 00:23:44,653
A hunter, scientist,
even Skinner has stealth.

216
00:23:44,654 --> 00:23:47,281
-Cheers.
-What of you?

217
00:23:47,282 --> 00:23:49,825
I have experience.

218
00:23:49,826 --> 00:23:55,498
Gray and I have met before,
many years ago at Eton College.

219
00:23:55,499 --> 00:23:57,375
A lecture, no doubt.

220
00:23:57,376 --> 00:24:01,837
You, the nation's hero,
Dorian, the eager listening boy.

221
00:24:01,838 --> 00:24:03,756
Quite the reverse.

222
00:24:03,757 --> 00:24:06,884
It was Gray visiting Eton...

223
00:24:08,428 --> 00:24:10,221
...and I was the boy.

224
00:24:17,854 --> 00:24:18,979
What is it?

225
00:24:30,909 --> 00:24:33,494
-Gray?
-They are not mine.

226
00:24:33,495 --> 00:24:35,538
They're mine.

227
00:24:41,837 --> 00:24:46,132
First meetings usually
warrant introductions.

228
00:24:46,133 --> 00:24:50,261
Of course. I am the Fantom.

229
00:24:50,262 --> 00:24:57,685
You are the League of so-called
Extraordinary Gentlemen.

230
00:24:57,686 --> 00:24:59,645
Introductions made.

231
00:24:59,646 --> 00:25:05,818
Oh, and I'm scarred, Mr. Quatermain,
not blind.

232
00:25:05,819 --> 00:25:06,986
Drop the gun.

233
00:25:10,532 --> 00:25:15,369
Your mission is to stop me.
That, of course, I cannot permit.

234
00:25:15,370 --> 00:25:19,457
So I give to you all a special
one-time invitation.

235
00:25:20,417 --> 00:25:21,834
Join me.

236
00:25:21,835 --> 00:25:25,087
You think we'll help start a war
that will consume the planet?

237
00:25:25,088 --> 00:25:28,841
While you profit
from your arms race?

238
00:25:28,842 --> 00:25:33,929
I cannot deny that fortunes
are made in war.

239
00:25:33,930 --> 00:25:38,726
Imagine the riches
a world war will yield.

240
00:25:38,727 --> 00:25:41,770
-He's not wrong.
-Speak for yourself.

241
00:26:50,590 --> 00:26:52,675
Draw your pistol!

242
00:26:52,676 --> 00:26:55,511
I walk a different path.

243
00:27:11,403 --> 00:27:12,987
Run, James!

244
00:27:25,792 --> 00:27:27,042
Hang on.

245
00:27:34,551 --> 00:27:36,135
Night-night.

246
00:28:00,744 --> 00:28:02,161
Dorian!

247
00:28:17,636 --> 00:28:20,012
What are you?

248
00:28:20,013 --> 00:28:22,056
I'm complicated.

249
00:28:44,287 --> 00:28:45,788
Damn.

250
00:28:47,123 --> 00:28:51,585
I thought I was special.
You're invulnerable to harm.

251
00:28:51,586 --> 00:28:54,421
I don't like to boast.
What happened to Mina?

252
00:28:54,422 --> 00:28:57,716
Oh, she's probably hip-deep
in some sort of trouble.

253
00:28:57,717 --> 00:29:01,136
Don't be such an alarmist, Mr. Q.

254
00:29:01,137 --> 00:29:03,806
And my hips
are none of your business.

255
00:29:08,436 --> 00:29:10,145
Shoot!

256
00:29:10,146 --> 00:29:11,939
Go on!

257
00:29:17,070 --> 00:29:20,823
I guessed as much, that they
would do anything to protect you.

258
00:29:20,824 --> 00:29:24,785
See, now that's your biggest mistake.

259
00:29:24,786 --> 00:29:27,621
Thinking that I need them
to protect me.

260
00:29:44,723 --> 00:29:47,266
Extraordinary.

261
00:29:48,810 --> 00:29:52,855
Boy, they told me European women
had funny ways.

262
00:29:54,899 --> 00:29:56,525
You missed a spot.

263
00:29:59,320 --> 00:30:01,155
Excuse me.

264
00:30:02,907 --> 00:30:04,116
And you are?

265
00:30:04,117 --> 00:30:07,828
Special Agent Sawyer
of the American Secret Service.

266
00:30:07,829 --> 00:30:10,372
Then America is aware
of the situation?

267
00:30:10,373 --> 00:30:15,127
If war starts in Europe, how long
will it take until it crosses the Atlantic?

268
00:30:15,128 --> 00:30:19,381
I followed you. I knocked out a straggler,
and I took his place.

269
00:30:19,382 --> 00:30:24,762
Very noble. But this is a private party,
and you're not invited.

270
00:30:24,763 --> 00:30:28,599
Actually, Dorian has declined...

271
00:30:28,600 --> 00:30:30,934
...so we are one shy of a full deck.

272
00:30:30,935 --> 00:30:35,481
On the contrary, the battle
was just the spur I needed.

273
00:30:35,482 --> 00:30:38,150
That and the thrill
of a friendship renewed.

274
00:30:40,779 --> 00:30:42,988
So you're not needed.

275
00:30:48,620 --> 00:30:50,662
-Winchester.
-That's right.

276
00:30:50,663 --> 00:30:54,166
-It's modified, American style.
-American-style shooting too.

277
00:30:55,210 --> 00:30:57,419
Well, whatever it takes.

278
00:30:58,797 --> 00:31:01,590
You like it, I brought two.

279
00:31:03,760 --> 00:31:05,719
You're in.

280
00:31:08,640 --> 00:31:12,893
-So, what's the next port of call?
-Paris.

281
00:31:12,894 --> 00:31:15,604
Just one last member to recruit.

282
00:31:15,605 --> 00:31:19,733
Capture is more the word,
and it will be quite a hunt.

283
00:31:19,734 --> 00:31:22,361
You make him sound like
some kind of animal.

284
00:31:22,362 --> 00:31:27,825
Oh, speaking thus, Mrs. Harker,
your conduct a moment ago...?

285
00:31:27,826 --> 00:31:33,038
Indeed. We are all aquiver
with curiosity.

286
00:31:35,416 --> 00:31:37,125
Well....

287
00:31:40,338 --> 00:31:43,507
My husband was Jonathan Harker.

288
00:31:43,508 --> 00:31:47,177
With a professor named Van Helsing,
we fought a dangerous evil.

289
00:31:48,304 --> 00:31:53,350
It had a name, Dracula.
He was Transylvanian.

290
00:31:53,351 --> 00:31:55,310
European?

291
00:31:55,311 --> 00:31:59,439
One of those radicals the newspapers
love to report on?

292
00:31:59,440 --> 00:32:01,984
I don't know, Mr. Skinner.

293
00:32:04,988 --> 00:32:09,908
Is the vampiric sucking of people's
blood radical behavior?

294
00:32:18,710 --> 00:32:21,837
Our transportation is forthcoming.

295
00:32:21,838 --> 00:32:24,006
A boat?

296
00:32:24,007 --> 00:32:27,843
It travels on water,
if that's what you mean.

297
00:32:31,806 --> 00:32:35,267
And beneath it.

298
00:32:53,995 --> 00:33:00,000
Behold Nautilus,
the Sword of the Ocean.

299
00:33:03,338 --> 00:33:06,298
Next stop, Paris.

300
00:33:15,975 --> 00:33:18,894
I don't see what we need
a big monkey for.

301
00:33:18,895 --> 00:33:23,523
Well, this big monkey has terrorized
the Rue Morgue for months.

302
00:33:23,524 --> 00:33:26,818
Imagine the mayhem
he'll give the enemy.

303
00:33:32,575 --> 00:33:35,452
-You missed!
-I'm not trying to hit him.

304
00:33:37,497 --> 00:33:39,331
Turn left, Mr. Hyde.

305
00:33:46,255 --> 00:33:48,882
Make him turn left, boy.

306
00:33:53,179 --> 00:33:56,723
If you can't do it with one bullet,
don't do it at all.

307
00:33:56,724 --> 00:34:00,185
-He's doubled back.
-Precisely. Come on.

308
00:34:04,774 --> 00:34:06,274
Look out!

309
00:34:08,486 --> 00:34:10,237
That was naughty.

310
00:34:12,532 --> 00:34:16,284
-Thanks.
-Eyes open, boy.

311
00:34:16,285 --> 00:34:18,662
I can't protect you all the time.

312
00:34:22,375 --> 00:34:24,751
He's afraid.

313
00:34:24,752 --> 00:34:27,045
I can't smell anything.

314
00:34:59,829 --> 00:35:01,538
Perfect.

315
00:35:01,539 --> 00:35:04,082
Welcome aboard, Mr. Hyde.

316
00:35:12,717 --> 00:35:17,054
"This hunt's too dangerous for a woman,
even one such as you. Leave it to me. "

317
00:35:24,103 --> 00:35:25,854
Good God.

318
00:35:35,198 --> 00:35:37,866
Hello, Dorian.

319
00:35:37,867 --> 00:35:41,203
The great white hunter's
bagged his prize.

320
00:35:42,997 --> 00:35:45,540
Or the prize bagged him.

321
00:35:49,003 --> 00:35:51,922
Stay back, if you value your life.

322
00:36:04,143 --> 00:36:07,270
-You scratched me.
-Better me than him.

323
00:36:10,483 --> 00:36:12,484
Well, this is nice.

324
00:36:13,820 --> 00:36:16,029
Mr. Hyde.

325
00:36:16,030 --> 00:36:18,990
You've done terrible things in England.

326
00:36:18,991 --> 00:36:23,703
So terrible that you fled the country.

327
00:36:23,704 --> 00:36:27,290
Now, I'm ashamed to say that
Her Majesty's government...

328
00:36:27,291 --> 00:36:31,545
... is willing to offer you amnesty...

329
00:36:31,546 --> 00:36:34,548
... in return for your services.

330
00:36:36,092 --> 00:36:38,426
Do you want to go home?

331
00:36:38,427 --> 00:36:40,929
Home?

332
00:36:40,930 --> 00:36:44,558
"Home's where the heart is,"
that's what they say.

333
00:36:44,559 --> 00:36:47,602
And I have been missing London so.

334
00:36:47,603 --> 00:36:51,940
Its sorrow is as sweet
to me as a rare wine.

335
00:36:55,820 --> 00:36:57,779
I am yours.

336
00:37:02,285 --> 00:37:03,827
Don't be afraid.

337
00:37:03,828 --> 00:37:07,372
-Who says I'm afraid?
-You do!

338
00:37:07,373 --> 00:37:09,499
You stink of fear!

339
00:37:12,795 --> 00:37:14,462
Quite the parlor trick.

340
00:37:16,007 --> 00:37:18,758
You wait till you see my next one.

341
00:38:09,143 --> 00:38:11,102
Dr. Jekyll...

342
00:38:11,103 --> 00:38:13,021
...at your service.

343
00:38:18,110 --> 00:38:19,736
So...

344
00:38:20,905 --> 00:38:22,280
...the League is set.

345
00:38:28,788 --> 00:38:30,789
So is the date for the conference.

346
00:38:30,790 --> 00:38:34,501
-We have three days.
-Three days?

347
00:38:34,502 --> 00:38:37,462
Can this canoe do that?

348
00:38:37,463 --> 00:38:42,050
You underestimate the Nautilus, sir.

349
00:38:42,051 --> 00:38:45,011
You underestimate her greatly.

350
00:39:13,833 --> 00:39:16,084
She's out of your league.

351
00:39:21,340 --> 00:39:22,590
Mina?

352
00:39:25,094 --> 00:39:28,179
Don't flatter yourself, Dorian.

353
00:39:31,225 --> 00:39:33,601
Fortune rewards the bold.

354
00:39:35,938 --> 00:39:41,443
If you require help during the voyage,
Mrs. Harker, just let me know.

355
00:39:41,444 --> 00:39:45,488
I'm curious how you think
you'll assist me, Agent Sawyer.

356
00:39:46,574 --> 00:39:48,116
Heavy lifting.

357
00:39:52,621 --> 00:39:55,081
Light banter.

358
00:39:55,082 --> 00:39:58,877
-I'm a useful kind of guy.
-You're sweet.

359
00:39:58,878 --> 00:40:00,795
And you're young.

360
00:40:00,796 --> 00:40:04,132
Neither are traits that I hold
in high regard.

361
00:40:11,057 --> 00:40:14,893
The solar panels are fully charged.
We'll be diving in a moment.

362
00:40:16,062 --> 00:40:18,229
Mr. Quatermain.

363
00:40:18,230 --> 00:40:21,066
Dr. Jekyll, Mr. Skinner.

364
00:40:21,067 --> 00:40:24,486
-Captain.
-Nemo.

365
00:40:54,225 --> 00:40:58,937
The plans the Fantom stole from
the Bank of England. These are copies.

366
00:40:58,938 --> 00:41:02,190
Da Vinci's blueprints of Venice.

367
00:41:05,361 --> 00:41:07,987
Its foundations...

368
00:41:07,988 --> 00:41:09,864
...and waterways.

369
00:41:12,076 --> 00:41:14,619
So he'll attack the conference by sea.

370
00:41:31,679 --> 00:41:34,472
Easy, Allan, easy.

371
00:41:34,473 --> 00:41:39,227
Skinner! I want you dressed
at all times!

372
00:41:40,521 --> 00:41:43,481
Or it's my boot up your ass!

373
00:41:51,282 --> 00:41:53,825
Dinner is served, Mr. Quatermain.

374
00:41:53,826 --> 00:41:56,244
I can offer you a jacket,
if you require.

375
00:41:56,245 --> 00:41:58,204
Oh, thank you, no.

376
00:41:58,205 --> 00:42:03,126
But thank you for your
contributions so far.

377
00:42:05,629 --> 00:42:09,591
I may have been overly rude earlier...

378
00:42:09,592 --> 00:42:11,926
...when I called you a pirate.

379
00:42:11,927 --> 00:42:16,681
And I may have been overly charitable
when I said I wasn't.

380
00:42:16,682 --> 00:42:19,225
But I try to live in the now...

381
00:42:19,226 --> 00:42:23,438
...where the ghosts of old wrongs
do not abide. What of you?

382
00:42:23,439 --> 00:42:26,733
I don't believe in ghosts,
although I've seen my share.

383
00:42:26,734 --> 00:42:29,319
Your past haunts you.

384
00:42:33,574 --> 00:42:36,409
Vanity, pride...

385
00:42:37,953 --> 00:42:42,916
... mistakes that cost me someone dear.

386
00:42:42,917 --> 00:42:47,003
So now you throw yourself
in harm's way?

387
00:42:47,004 --> 00:42:51,257
Old tigers, sensing the end,
they're at their most fierce.

388
00:42:52,968 --> 00:42:54,719
And they go down fighting.

389
00:43:17,743 --> 00:43:20,286
They're not how I left them, captain.

390
00:43:21,789 --> 00:43:23,748
That's all I'm saying.

391
00:43:27,336 --> 00:43:29,045
Powder?

392
00:43:30,297 --> 00:43:33,049
You think sabotage?

393
00:43:33,050 --> 00:43:34,968
We ain't that far off course.

394
00:43:55,197 --> 00:43:57,115
Did you want something?

395
00:43:57,116 --> 00:44:00,702
No, I was just wondering why
you signed up for all this.

396
00:44:02,830 --> 00:44:05,915
Nemo told me you hate
the British Empire.

397
00:44:05,916 --> 00:44:08,293
They called, and I answered.

398
00:44:08,294 --> 00:44:10,962
Yeah, well that ain't all of it,
though, is it?

399
00:44:17,428 --> 00:44:18,845
Sorry I asked.

400
00:44:28,897 --> 00:44:30,732
A few years ago...

401
00:44:30,733 --> 00:44:35,111
...the British approached me with
a mission for queen and country.

402
00:44:35,112 --> 00:44:38,156
That sounds like the morning ride
to work for you.

403
00:44:38,157 --> 00:44:41,451
I signed on without hesitation.

404
00:44:41,452 --> 00:44:43,786
I even took my son along.

405
00:44:45,122 --> 00:44:49,167
I led and my son followed.

406
00:44:50,127 --> 00:44:52,837
He died in my arms.

407
00:44:56,383 --> 00:45:02,138
After that, I washed my hands
of England and the Empire and...

408
00:45:02,139 --> 00:45:07,810
...the legend of
Allan-bloody-Quatermain.

409
00:45:17,112 --> 00:45:19,072
Now...

410
00:45:19,073 --> 00:45:21,699
...would you like to learn how to shoot?

411
00:45:23,243 --> 00:45:27,038
-I can already.
-I saw, it was very American.

412
00:45:27,039 --> 00:45:31,542
Fire enough bullets and hope
to hit the target.

413
00:45:31,543 --> 00:45:35,922
Sawyer, I'm talking about
pipping the ace...

414
00:45:35,923 --> 00:45:38,424
...at 900 yards.

415
00:45:40,844 --> 00:45:42,136
Try.

416
00:45:45,015 --> 00:45:48,184
Easy, easy.

417
00:45:56,443 --> 00:45:58,569
-Aim.
-That's easy.

418
00:45:58,570 --> 00:46:00,697
Allow for wind, target movement.

419
00:46:00,698 --> 00:46:04,534
-Yeah, that's easy too.
-This is the part that's not.

420
00:46:04,535 --> 00:46:07,370
You have to feel the shot.

421
00:46:07,371 --> 00:46:10,123
Take your time with it.

422
00:46:10,124 --> 00:46:12,834
You have all the time you need.

423
00:46:12,835 --> 00:46:15,962
All the time in the world.

424
00:46:17,131 --> 00:46:19,298
Take your....

425
00:46:21,343 --> 00:46:23,344
Too soon.

426
00:46:23,345 --> 00:46:27,765
But that was bloody close.
And at 500 yards too.

427
00:46:27,766 --> 00:46:29,308
Again.

428
00:46:38,193 --> 00:46:41,070
Did you teach your son
to shoot like this?

429
00:46:46,160 --> 00:46:47,744
Quatermain?

430
00:47:29,620 --> 00:47:32,205
That's Kali.

431
00:47:32,206 --> 00:47:34,499
Goddess of death.

432
00:47:35,709 --> 00:47:39,170
Nemo worships death,
can we trust him?

433
00:47:41,298 --> 00:47:43,966
He's not the one I'm worried about.

434
00:48:06,782 --> 00:48:08,491
Mina.

435
00:48:09,827 --> 00:48:13,204
I'm identifying a powder
that Nemo found.

436
00:48:15,123 --> 00:48:17,834
Magnesium phosphorus.

437
00:48:17,835 --> 00:48:20,962
Photographers use it to create a flash.

438
00:48:20,963 --> 00:48:22,505
A camera, perhaps?

439
00:48:22,506 --> 00:48:26,342
It appears that someone wishes
to capture this vessel's secrets.

440
00:48:33,308 --> 00:48:37,311
I was surprised you ultimately
joined the League.

441
00:48:39,648 --> 00:48:44,277
I mean to undo the flaws
in my character.

442
00:48:44,278 --> 00:48:46,821
I want to face my demons.

443
00:48:46,822 --> 00:48:49,198
And what do you know of demons?

444
00:48:54,413 --> 00:48:58,666
Do you recall a space
on the wall of my home?

445
00:48:58,667 --> 00:49:01,043
A picture was missing.

446
00:49:01,962 --> 00:49:04,755
Although the picture is my portrait...

447
00:49:05,924 --> 00:49:09,510
... I doubt you'd recognize
the face upon it.

448
00:49:09,511 --> 00:49:14,223
Every year that passes,
my portrait ages...

449
00:49:14,224 --> 00:49:16,350
... instead of me.

450
00:49:17,728 --> 00:49:20,146
When did you last see it?

451
00:49:22,399 --> 00:49:25,359
I dare not look upon it myself...

452
00:49:25,360 --> 00:49:28,821
...or the magic of the painting
would be undone.

453
00:49:39,625 --> 00:49:41,250
Nightcap?

454
00:49:41,251 --> 00:49:43,794
I'm not much of a drinker.

455
00:49:55,933 --> 00:49:58,434
Just a small one then.

456
00:50:05,651 --> 00:50:07,485
How clumsy of me.

457
00:50:07,486 --> 00:50:09,737
We don't want blood everywhere.

458
00:50:11,031 --> 00:50:13,741
-Do we?
-No.

459
00:50:13,742 --> 00:50:15,117
Not blood.

460
00:50:21,458 --> 00:50:25,378
Yes, Henry. Look, but don't touch.

461
00:50:26,380 --> 00:50:28,506
That's your way.

462
00:50:29,633 --> 00:50:31,968
Just shut up.

463
00:50:31,969 --> 00:50:34,220
-I won't be tricked again.
-Tricked?

464
00:50:34,221 --> 00:50:37,765
You've known what I was about
each time you drank the formula.

465
00:50:37,766 --> 00:50:40,059
Liar! I'm a good man, a good man!

466
00:50:40,060 --> 00:50:42,812
Well, who's Iying now?
You want it!

467
00:50:42,813 --> 00:50:46,774
Even more than you want her.
You can't shut me out forever!

468
00:50:46,775 --> 00:50:48,442
-Drink the elixir!
-No!

469
00:50:48,443 --> 00:50:50,736
-She barely even looks at you!
-Be quiet!

470
00:50:52,322 --> 00:50:54,281
She looked at me!

471
00:50:56,952 --> 00:50:59,453
Contain your evil, doctor!

472
00:51:00,789 --> 00:51:04,375
I'll not have the brute free
upon my ship.

473
00:51:04,376 --> 00:51:06,210
Must I take drastic steps?

474
00:51:06,211 --> 00:51:09,714
-I am in control.
-I very much doubt it.

475
00:51:09,715 --> 00:51:14,760
Your talk is all well and good, sir,
but your own past is far from laudable.

476
00:51:33,488 --> 00:51:34,739
Let me out.

477
00:51:38,368 --> 00:51:42,872
If I didn't know better,
I'd swear I already had.

478
00:51:44,332 --> 00:51:46,709
One of the vials is missing.

479
00:51:48,086 --> 00:51:49,920
Oh, God.

480
00:51:49,921 --> 00:51:56,343
So it's clear. With da Vinci's blueprints
and enough explosives...

481
00:51:56,344 --> 00:52:00,598
... he could set a bomb that would
blow Venice's foundations to rubble.

482
00:52:00,599 --> 00:52:05,519
-He's gonna sink the whole city.
-Yes, and spark off his world war.

483
00:52:06,521 --> 00:52:09,398
I'm afraid that's not the sum
of our problems.

484
00:52:12,069 --> 00:52:16,947
Skinner, he's taken a vial
of my formula.

485
00:52:16,948 --> 00:52:19,825
-Are you sure?
-Who else?

486
00:52:19,826 --> 00:52:23,079
You've seen the way
the sneaky blackguard operates.

487
00:53:10,544 --> 00:53:13,003
It's the carnival.

488
00:53:13,004 --> 00:53:15,548
My God, we must locate that bomb.

489
00:54:17,152 --> 00:54:19,403
We can go no further, captain.

490
00:54:19,404 --> 00:54:20,863
All ahead, stop.

491
00:54:46,640 --> 00:54:48,849
Deploy the divers beneath the city.

492
00:54:48,850 --> 00:54:52,102
-The explosives must be found.
-Aye, aye, sir.

493
00:54:52,103 --> 00:54:55,606
The city is vast. It could be anywhere.

494
00:54:55,607 --> 00:54:58,776
-What about Skinner?
-I'd be alert for his treachery.

495
00:55:11,122 --> 00:55:13,165
Bloody carnival.

496
00:55:13,166 --> 00:55:15,876
God, I feared the worst.

497
00:55:15,877 --> 00:55:17,628
It's all right. We still have-

498
00:55:28,807 --> 00:55:32,810
-The buildings are falling like dominoes!
-We're too late!

499
00:55:34,729 --> 00:55:37,439
There must be more than one bomb.

500
00:55:37,440 --> 00:55:39,441
Nemo!

501
00:55:39,442 --> 00:55:43,445
The bombs are at the city's center.
We must take out one key building.

502
00:55:43,446 --> 00:55:47,032
-Get ahead and destroy the next building!
-Interrupt the chain!

503
00:55:47,033 --> 00:55:51,704
With a beacon at the coordinates, I could
launch a rocket and take out the domino.

504
00:55:51,705 --> 00:55:54,915
That's ridiculous!
We'd never get there in time.

505
00:55:54,916 --> 00:55:58,836
-No room for error, but it could work.
-What are you talking about?

506
00:55:58,837 --> 00:56:03,215
I'm an immortal, sir, not a gazelle.
How can we outrun this?

507
00:56:10,390 --> 00:56:12,266
Care for a spin?

508
00:56:16,229 --> 00:56:17,730
Come on, Jekyll, get in!

509
00:56:17,731 --> 00:56:20,232
-I'll need coordinates.
-Can you track this?

510
00:56:20,233 --> 00:56:22,401
-Of course.
-Then this is the target.

511
00:56:22,402 --> 00:56:24,611
-Launch when you see the flare.
-Right.

512
00:56:24,612 --> 00:56:28,532
-Jekyll, come on, we'll need Hyde.
-No!

513
00:56:28,533 --> 00:56:30,993
Hyde will never use me again.

514
00:56:30,994 --> 00:56:33,287
Then what good are you?

515
00:56:46,343 --> 00:56:48,552
Straight ahead, then turn left.

516
00:56:48,553 --> 00:56:51,138
No, no, take a right
after the canal ports.

517
00:56:51,139 --> 00:56:53,766
We must turn left to get ahead of the-

518
00:56:56,102 --> 00:56:57,478
Snipers!

519
00:56:58,980 --> 00:57:01,815
Damn Skinner, he must have
told them we were coming.

520
00:57:18,124 --> 00:57:19,792
Dorian!

521
00:57:24,339 --> 00:57:27,007
-Can't get a clear shot!
-Then take the wheel!

522
00:57:28,259 --> 00:57:29,927
Sawyer!

523
00:57:34,349 --> 00:57:38,310
Sit down, you buffoon. I don't know
how to drive this bloody thing!

524
00:57:39,354 --> 00:57:41,063
Get down!

525
00:57:42,190 --> 00:57:43,690
Got it!

526
00:57:43,691 --> 00:57:47,194
Save your bullets. These men are mine!

527
00:57:49,697 --> 00:57:51,365
Did you see that?

528
00:57:53,660 --> 00:57:57,162
-Did you see what she just did?
-Keep your eyes on the road!

529
00:58:05,713 --> 00:58:08,632
It's a gauntlet!

530
00:58:08,633 --> 00:58:11,593
The vampire lady has us covered!

531
00:58:43,168 --> 00:58:46,920
The collapse has reached the carnival.
Time's running out.

532
00:58:46,921 --> 00:58:49,256
Tune the tracer to the car's frequency.

533
00:58:49,257 --> 00:58:52,217
-The rocket must be ready to launch.
-Aye, aye, captain.

534
00:59:10,862 --> 00:59:13,238
Come on, Sawyer, come on!

535
00:59:13,239 --> 00:59:16,283
-Captain, we must pull out!
-No, we stay and do our job!

536
00:59:23,625 --> 00:59:26,210
Remember the flare. I'm off!

537
00:59:27,337 --> 00:59:30,339
-What?
-Don't forget, you're the target.

538
00:59:53,738 --> 00:59:56,990
-We'll be smashed apart!
-Nemo says we stay, we stay.

539
01:00:05,333 --> 01:00:07,876
He's overtaken the collapse!
Be ready to launch!

540
01:00:22,850 --> 01:00:23,934
Launch!

541
01:00:36,364 --> 01:00:37,906
Bravo, boy.

542
01:01:02,348 --> 01:01:04,391
He's done it.

543
01:01:27,957 --> 01:01:30,792
Venice still stands.

544
01:01:36,966 --> 01:01:38,967
You've failed.

545
01:01:45,058 --> 01:01:48,602
You see yourself as the brave
John Bull.

546
01:01:51,022 --> 01:01:53,398
But you're weak.

547
01:01:55,026 --> 01:01:59,071
You run from the memory
of your son's death.

548
01:01:59,072 --> 01:02:02,532
You should have trained him
a little better.

549
01:02:06,079 --> 01:02:10,457
You may as well have put the gun to
his head and pulled the trigger yourself.

550
01:02:13,836 --> 01:02:16,797
Oh, yes, I know all about you.

551
01:02:19,467 --> 01:02:22,594
I know all about your spy.

552
01:02:22,595 --> 01:02:24,388
Do you?

553
01:02:28,935 --> 01:02:31,478
Mr. Gray? What happened?

554
01:02:31,479 --> 01:02:34,272
Misadventure. Have the others returned?

555
01:02:34,273 --> 01:02:37,776
You're the first, hopefully not the last.

556
01:02:37,777 --> 01:02:41,697
That bastard, Skinner.
A lot to answer for.

557
01:02:41,698 --> 01:02:45,033
Skinner? No.

558
01:02:45,034 --> 01:02:46,576
Me.

559
01:03:06,514 --> 01:03:07,681
You?

560
01:03:09,308 --> 01:03:11,393
You don't know the half of it.

561
01:03:25,867 --> 01:03:28,577
The Fantom is M.

562
01:03:29,996 --> 01:03:32,205
And the hunt is still on.

563
01:03:32,206 --> 01:03:35,000
-What are you talking about?
-The Fantom is M...

564
01:03:35,001 --> 01:03:37,669
-...the man who recruited us.
-Let me see that.

565
01:03:37,670 --> 01:03:40,756
-But how?
-We'll get our answers.

566
01:03:40,757 --> 01:03:44,593
-Where are the others?
-Dorian's missing in action.

567
01:03:44,594 --> 01:03:49,598
And Mr. Skinner must have fled
when he realized we knew.

568
01:03:51,350 --> 01:03:53,226
And Sawyer?

569
01:03:53,227 --> 01:03:56,146
He'll live to fight another day.

570
01:04:01,903 --> 01:04:06,323
Don't worry, I've had my fill of throats
for this evening.

571
01:04:09,285 --> 01:04:11,286
Captain!

572
01:04:19,295 --> 01:04:22,088
It was Gray...

573
01:04:22,089 --> 01:04:23,840
... not Skinner.

574
01:04:26,177 --> 01:04:28,970
Gray has tricked us all.

575
01:04:41,192 --> 01:04:42,943
What is it?

576
01:04:42,944 --> 01:04:45,403
The sound of treachery!

577
01:05:00,336 --> 01:05:02,295
What is that thing?

578
01:05:02,296 --> 01:05:04,840
My exploration pod.

579
01:05:22,316 --> 01:05:26,987
-We could track it.
-I intend to catch it!

580
01:06:06,235 --> 01:06:08,695
That is us.

581
01:06:08,696 --> 01:06:10,906
That is the Nautiloid.

582
01:06:10,907 --> 01:06:13,950
We will be upon them soon.

583
01:06:13,951 --> 01:06:16,453
You okay?

584
01:06:16,454 --> 01:06:19,372
Just a little shaken.

585
01:06:29,884 --> 01:06:31,718
Nemo?

586
01:06:31,719 --> 01:06:34,095
It's nothing of mine.

587
01:06:36,933 --> 01:06:40,685
Captain, the noise came from this.

588
01:06:40,686 --> 01:06:42,687
A recording disk?

589
01:06:51,322 --> 01:06:53,490
Ready, professor?

590
01:06:55,117 --> 01:06:56,660
Recording.

591
01:06:59,163 --> 01:07:01,915
Gentlemen, if you're hearing this...

592
01:07:01,916 --> 01:07:05,085
...then every step leading up to it
has gone as planned.

593
01:07:05,086 --> 01:07:07,712
And I have been true
to the goals set to me.

594
01:07:07,713 --> 01:07:09,506
Yes, it's me, Dorian.

595
01:07:09,507 --> 01:07:12,759
As you know by now,
I'm no loyal son of the Empire.

596
01:07:12,760 --> 01:07:16,471
My loyalty to Mr. M comes
in no small part from his possession...

597
01:07:16,472 --> 01:07:19,766
...of something I hold very dear
to my heart...

598
01:07:19,767 --> 01:07:22,352
...something I would do anything
to regain.

599
01:07:22,353 --> 01:07:25,105
-Are you all right?
-My ears hurt. It's nothing.

600
01:07:25,106 --> 01:07:27,899
Everything so far
has been misdirection.

601
01:07:27,900 --> 01:07:30,902
Sanderson Reed,
the assassins in Kenya...

602
01:07:30,903 --> 01:07:34,948
...your recruitment and mission,
the secret conference, a myth.

603
01:07:34,949 --> 01:07:39,035
Even the League itself. There is
no League. There never was.

604
01:07:39,036 --> 01:07:41,871
It was a ruse to get me closer
to my goals.

605
01:07:41,872 --> 01:07:44,040
He likes the sound of his own voice.

606
01:07:44,041 --> 01:07:47,711
You see, I want you, each of you,
even tired old Quatermain.

607
01:07:47,712 --> 01:07:50,463
He'll capture Hyde
where all others have failed.

608
01:07:50,464 --> 01:07:53,550
The question is why.
Why all this mask and mystery?

609
01:07:53,551 --> 01:07:58,304
Because in the war to come, I intend
to wield the greatest weapon of all:

610
01:07:58,305 --> 01:08:00,682
The power of the League itself.

611
01:08:00,683 --> 01:08:04,561
Turn it off, Henry.
Please, turn it off.

612
01:08:04,562 --> 01:08:08,815
To that end, I set my wolf
among you sheep...

613
01:08:08,816 --> 01:08:10,275
Growl.

614
01:08:10,276 --> 01:08:13,278
...who'll gain your trust,
quietly lead you astray.

615
01:08:13,279 --> 01:08:17,157
And all the while, I'll collect you,
the parts of you I need.

616
01:08:17,158 --> 01:08:19,242
Nemo's science.

617
01:08:19,243 --> 01:08:20,744
Magnesium phosphorus.

618
01:08:20,745 --> 01:08:22,996
Skinner's skin sample.

619
01:08:22,997 --> 01:08:25,665
You scratched me.

620
01:08:25,666 --> 01:08:27,917
Jekyll's potion.

621
01:08:29,587 --> 01:08:32,255
And Mina's blood.

622
01:08:34,258 --> 01:08:37,844
He's stolen us, and we let him.

623
01:08:37,845 --> 01:08:41,306
If you fail to save Venice,
then I get my war.

624
01:08:41,307 --> 01:08:43,183
And if you succeed...

625
01:08:43,184 --> 01:08:47,062
...well, it's a small price to pay
for Gray to go about his task.

626
01:08:47,063 --> 01:08:50,065
War will come, sooner or later...

627
01:08:50,066 --> 01:08:52,609
...as inevitable as summer into autumn.

628
01:08:52,610 --> 01:08:56,321
Now, some of you, perhaps Quatermain
if he isn't dead...

629
01:08:56,322 --> 01:08:59,491
...will pause to ask why I'm letting
you know all this.

630
01:08:59,492 --> 01:09:02,494
What fool reveals his stratagem
before the game is over?

631
01:09:04,038 --> 01:09:07,165
It is over, for you...

632
01:09:07,166 --> 01:09:10,293
...because my voice isn't
the only sound being made.

633
01:09:10,294 --> 01:09:15,548
While I've rambled on, a secondary layer
of inaudible sound...

634
01:09:15,549 --> 01:09:19,761
...higher than humans can hear,
audible to dogs and lower animals...

635
01:09:19,762 --> 01:09:25,433
...has been heard by crystal sensors
dotted about your vessel.

636
01:09:25,434 --> 01:09:28,603
Sensors attached to bombs.

637
01:09:30,397 --> 01:09:32,148
"Bomb" voyage.

638
01:10:18,904 --> 01:10:20,822
Out of the way!

639
01:10:20,823 --> 01:10:23,408
We have to surface!

640
01:10:23,409 --> 01:10:26,494
We're taking in water!
Controls are not responding!

641
01:10:26,495 --> 01:10:30,665
Primary engine room almost full!
Aft bulkhead open. Pump valves jammed!

642
01:10:30,666 --> 01:10:32,876
-Seal it off!
-There are men in there!

643
01:10:32,877 --> 01:10:36,504
For the greater good, we must seal it!

644
01:10:47,141 --> 01:10:49,100
We can do it, Henry.

645
01:10:49,101 --> 01:10:50,894
What are you talking about?

646
01:10:50,895 --> 01:10:54,439
You know we can do it, together!

647
01:11:04,700 --> 01:11:06,993
Seal it! Seal it up!

648
01:11:06,994 --> 01:11:09,412
Wait!

649
01:11:09,413 --> 01:11:12,498
Don't close it!

650
01:11:12,499 --> 01:11:15,001
-What are you doing?
-It's our last chance.

651
01:11:15,002 --> 01:11:17,253
But I'm ordered to close it!

652
01:11:17,254 --> 01:11:18,963
Then close it.

653
01:11:20,758 --> 01:11:22,133
Seal it up.

654
01:12:37,293 --> 01:12:40,128
Bravo, Edward. Bravo!

655
01:13:12,077 --> 01:13:16,539
Let's not make a saint out of a sinner.
Next time, he may not be so helpful.

656
01:13:16,540 --> 01:13:19,584
Can we still follow Gray?

657
01:13:20,586 --> 01:13:25,006
Well, we were the faster,
but now we're the tortoise to his hare.

658
01:13:25,007 --> 01:13:28,593
-So we're done?
-No, we're alive.

659
01:13:28,594 --> 01:13:32,472
If M has any ideas to the contrary,
that gives us an edge.

660
01:13:33,515 --> 01:13:35,516
We're getting a signal.

661
01:13:38,312 --> 01:13:39,645
Morse code.

662
01:13:39,646 --> 01:13:42,357
-What's it say?
-"Hello, my freaky darlings. "

663
01:13:42,358 --> 01:13:43,775
Skinner?

664
01:13:43,776 --> 01:13:48,321
"Hiding onboard little fish
with Gray and M.

665
01:13:48,322 --> 01:13:54,243
On way to base, east by northeast.
Follow my lead. "

666
01:14:48,549 --> 01:14:50,800
Good work...

667
01:14:50,801 --> 01:14:52,677
...all of you.

668
01:14:52,678 --> 01:14:55,513
Captain?

669
01:14:55,514 --> 01:14:57,390
All ahead, full!

670
01:15:12,781 --> 01:15:15,283
If Skinner's headings are correct...

671
01:15:15,284 --> 01:15:19,036
...we will pass through
the treacherous Straits of Tartary...

672
01:15:19,037 --> 01:15:21,956
...and enter the Amur River,
which empties out...

673
01:15:21,957 --> 01:15:28,546
...into the frozen lakes of Mongolia,
virtually inaccessible to outsiders.

674
01:15:57,993 --> 01:16:00,495
Peasant settlements.

675
01:16:00,496 --> 01:16:02,371
They're all deserted.

676
01:16:02,372 --> 01:16:04,790
Why deserted?

677
01:16:07,669 --> 01:16:10,004
Fear, no doubt.

678
01:16:39,868 --> 01:16:42,495
M's summer retreat.

679
01:16:42,496 --> 01:16:45,456
This is where Skinner signaled
he'd meet us.

680
01:16:45,457 --> 01:16:47,208
So we wait.

681
01:17:12,359 --> 01:17:14,569
Skinner?

682
01:17:54,568 --> 01:17:58,029
-We heard a noise.
-It was nothing.

683
01:18:06,038 --> 01:18:09,332
Just an old tiger sensing his end.

684
01:18:09,333 --> 01:18:13,628
Perhaps this was not his time
to die after all.

685
01:18:18,425 --> 01:18:22,136
-I've been waiting all week to do that.
-Get a grip, man.

686
01:18:22,137 --> 01:18:24,555
Well, I thought I just did.

687
01:18:25,891 --> 01:18:27,683
Mina.

688
01:18:27,684 --> 01:18:30,895
-Report!
-Well, hello to you too.

689
01:18:30,896 --> 01:18:34,440
And need I remind you,
I am naked in the snow.

690
01:18:34,441 --> 01:18:39,779
I can't feel any of my extremities,
and I mean any of them.

691
01:18:44,785 --> 01:18:46,994
With all the suspicion on the ship...

692
01:18:46,995 --> 01:18:49,914
... I knew you wouldn't believe
I wasn't the spy.

693
01:18:49,915 --> 01:18:52,958
I thought it was best to disappear.

694
01:18:52,959 --> 01:18:55,002
So, what are we dealing with?

695
01:18:55,003 --> 01:18:57,254
The fortress is vast.

696
01:18:57,255 --> 01:19:02,051
Furnaces produce iron for making
M's weapons of destruction.

697
01:19:11,645 --> 01:19:16,232
They're pieced together on the factory
floor by a private army of ruthless men...

698
01:19:16,233 --> 01:19:18,442
...who share his vision.

699
01:19:20,737 --> 01:19:24,156
But the worst was to come.

700
01:19:24,157 --> 01:19:30,621
In the dry dock, M's best minds
pervert Nemo's dream.

701
01:19:32,541 --> 01:19:36,001
-The Nautilus?
-Nautili. There's eight, for now.

702
01:19:36,002 --> 01:19:40,005
-What about the kidnapped scientists?
-M holds their families hostage.

703
01:19:40,006 --> 01:19:43,926
The men work,
or the women and children die.

704
01:19:44,803 --> 01:19:47,680
-Monstrous!
-That isn't the half of it.

705
01:19:47,681 --> 01:19:52,601
The scientists are forced to work night
and day to make new versions of us.

706
01:19:52,602 --> 01:19:56,814
Invisible spies, an army of Hydes,
vampiric assassins.

707
01:19:56,815 --> 01:20:00,276
M leaves for Europe today
with a sample of the chemicals...

708
01:20:00,277 --> 01:20:03,529
...to sell to the most eager nation.

709
01:20:03,530 --> 01:20:08,117
-I'll not let my evil infect the world.
-Do you think any of us feel differently?

710
01:20:08,118 --> 01:20:10,202
Chimney pipes laced to buildings...

711
01:20:10,203 --> 01:20:13,539
...and bombs in the furnaces
would make quite a bang.

712
01:20:13,540 --> 01:20:18,669
But someone needs to blow that place
to hell, and I am least likely to be seen.

713
01:20:18,670 --> 01:20:23,466
Skinner, I didn't know that you were
such a bare-faced liar.

714
01:20:23,467 --> 01:20:27,052
All that time pretending
that you weren't a hero?

715
01:20:27,053 --> 01:20:29,680
Shut up, or I'll come to my senses.

716
01:20:29,681 --> 01:20:35,060
And besides, any more like me,
and I'll lose the franchise.

717
01:20:37,063 --> 01:20:41,817
No, this cannot be a hunt
to the death, more's the pity.

718
01:20:41,818 --> 01:20:46,155
We must take M alive
if his secrets are to be uncovered.

719
01:20:46,156 --> 01:20:48,240
Not Gray.

720
01:20:48,241 --> 01:20:50,201
He's lived long enough.

721
01:20:50,202 --> 01:20:53,454
-Oh, I'll handle him.
-No.

722
01:20:54,873 --> 01:20:57,917
Nemo, you and Hyde
will free the prisoners.

723
01:20:57,918 --> 01:21:00,753
Sawyer and I will deal with M.

724
01:21:01,797 --> 01:21:03,506
Then the game is on.

725
01:21:05,926 --> 01:21:08,344
Do you mind?

726
01:21:10,430 --> 01:21:12,515
Night-night.

727
01:21:20,440 --> 01:21:23,442
Your painting's in your room.

728
01:21:23,443 --> 01:21:28,989
In return for the League, that was our
deal, and I'm glad to see you honor it.

729
01:21:28,990 --> 01:21:30,950
So, what now?

730
01:21:30,951 --> 01:21:35,746
London. I've had my fill of violence.
Now I'm in the mood for vice.

731
01:21:35,747 --> 01:21:39,375
You could stay, share my dream.

732
01:21:42,003 --> 01:21:46,382
I've lived long enough to see the future
become history, professor.

733
01:21:46,383 --> 01:21:51,220
Empires crumble.
There are no exceptions.

734
01:21:51,221 --> 01:21:53,848
You think you're better than me.

735
01:21:53,849 --> 01:21:59,520
You forget, I've seen your painting.

736
01:22:57,412 --> 01:23:00,122
Skinner said turn right at the column.

737
01:23:00,123 --> 01:23:02,041
Oh, great. Which one?

738
01:23:03,668 --> 01:23:05,753
This way.

739
01:23:08,882 --> 01:23:11,342
You lead, and I'll follow.

740
01:23:14,179 --> 01:23:16,055
The scientists.

741
01:23:16,056 --> 01:23:18,474
They're for Nemo.

742
01:23:32,822 --> 01:23:37,117
Prepare the armaments for shipment.
Prepare the armaments for shipment.

743
01:23:37,118 --> 01:23:39,078
They're moving out.

744
01:23:39,079 --> 01:23:41,497
Let's hope Skinner's up to the task.

745
01:23:41,498 --> 01:23:43,040
You, help him out!

746
01:24:10,986 --> 01:24:13,529
Release them!

747
01:24:13,530 --> 01:24:14,989
Quietly!

748
01:24:15,907 --> 01:24:18,283
Get them back to the ship.

749
01:24:57,907 --> 01:25:00,951
Intruders! Intruders!

750
01:25:00,952 --> 01:25:02,661
Intruders!

751
01:25:20,847 --> 01:25:25,476
James, here's your box of tricks!

752
01:25:27,896 --> 01:25:30,898
The brute's potion,
the vampire's blood...

753
01:25:30,899 --> 01:25:35,903
...the Indian's science
and mounted samples of invisible skin.

754
01:25:35,904 --> 01:25:38,113
They'll be all the rage in Europe.

755
01:25:38,114 --> 01:25:40,365
The Nautiloid is fueled and ready.

756
01:25:42,911 --> 01:25:47,623
-We have trouble.
-Trouble? I call it sport.

757
01:25:47,624 --> 01:25:52,586
Intruders! Indians! I think it's
Captain Nemo and his men!

758
01:25:52,587 --> 01:25:54,463
The prisoners are escaping!

759
01:25:54,464 --> 01:25:57,549
How many times do I have
to kill these cretins?

760
01:25:57,550 --> 01:25:59,760
-Make this the last.
-Damn them.

761
01:26:23,868 --> 01:26:26,787
Nemo, get the scientists!

762
01:26:28,206 --> 01:26:30,124
Is that all you've got?

763
01:26:56,860 --> 01:26:59,778
Do not move, M.

764
01:27:00,822 --> 01:27:06,869
Or would you prefer
Professor James Moriarty?

765
01:27:07,787 --> 01:27:12,082
James Moriarty,
the so-called Napoleon of crime?

766
01:27:12,083 --> 01:27:17,379
That man died at Reichenbach Falls.
He died, and I was reborn.

767
01:27:20,925 --> 01:27:23,427
Watch out!

768
01:27:23,428 --> 01:27:26,305
Eyes open, boy.
Can't protect you all of the time.

769
01:27:43,656 --> 01:27:46,283
Hello, lover.

770
01:27:53,583 --> 01:27:55,125
Mina.

771
01:27:58,379 --> 01:27:59,838
You're alive.

772
01:27:59,839 --> 01:28:02,799
It's possible I can't die.

773
01:28:05,386 --> 01:28:08,347
The same could be said of you.

774
01:28:09,807 --> 01:28:12,935
Let's put it to the test.

775
01:28:18,358 --> 01:28:21,777
-Minx.
-Do you realize what you've done?

776
01:28:21,778 --> 01:28:23,570
What you've let out of me?

777
01:28:23,571 --> 01:28:26,073
A woman's wrath. Oh, I'm petrified!

778
01:28:34,832 --> 01:28:36,792
We'll be at this all day.

779
01:28:49,806 --> 01:28:55,018
The bedroom, Mina, does it give
you memories or ideas?

780
01:28:55,019 --> 01:28:56,270
Ideas.

781
01:28:58,648 --> 01:29:03,777
If that had been permanent,
I'd have been very upset.

782
01:29:03,778 --> 01:29:05,988
Your painting.

783
01:29:12,328 --> 01:29:15,956
I hoped I'd get to nail you
one more time.

784
01:29:15,957 --> 01:29:18,667
I didn't think it'd be literally.

785
01:29:34,058 --> 01:29:36,226
Skinner?

786
01:29:38,021 --> 01:29:41,648
It's okay. It's Skinner.
What the hell are you doing here?

787
01:29:41,649 --> 01:29:43,734
What makes you think I'm Skinner?

788
01:30:50,468 --> 01:30:51,968
Sawyer!

789
01:30:51,969 --> 01:30:53,970
Run for it!

790
01:31:02,688 --> 01:31:04,231
Keep moving!

791
01:31:04,232 --> 01:31:06,775
Go over! Go, quickly!

792
01:31:20,206 --> 01:31:23,083
Come on! You want more?

793
01:31:25,378 --> 01:31:27,421
No!

794
01:31:29,924 --> 01:31:32,676
Not the whole thing.

795
01:31:52,655 --> 01:31:55,365
You are so lovely.

796
01:32:07,462 --> 01:32:09,546
Thank you.

797
01:32:11,966 --> 01:32:14,843
You broke my heart once.

798
01:32:14,844 --> 01:32:17,721
This time you missed.

799
01:32:17,722 --> 01:32:21,600
You spoke once of wanting
to meet your demon.

800
01:32:29,859 --> 01:32:33,403
Here he is.

801
01:32:56,594 --> 01:32:58,803
Skinner?

802
01:33:01,933 --> 01:33:05,060
That's the last time I play
with matches.

803
01:33:29,585 --> 01:33:31,086
End of the line, Moriarty.

804
01:33:40,471 --> 01:33:41,471
To the death...

805
01:33:42,390 --> 01:33:45,809
-... but you'll need Hyde to make it mine.
-He'll be making his own fun.

806
01:33:51,023 --> 01:33:53,024
Nemo!

807
01:33:56,862 --> 01:33:59,781
Here!

808
01:33:59,782 --> 01:34:02,075
Nemo!

809
01:34:10,209 --> 01:34:12,586
Nemo, run!

810
01:34:31,856 --> 01:34:33,732
What is that thing?

811
01:34:33,733 --> 01:34:36,651
It's me on a bad day.

812
01:35:27,453 --> 01:35:30,330
He's too strong. We're trapped.

813
01:35:30,331 --> 01:35:33,958
No, he's burning through the formula
at an accelerated rate.

814
01:35:37,755 --> 01:35:40,465
He'll soon change back.

815
01:35:41,509 --> 01:35:43,343
Nemo!

816
01:35:59,568 --> 01:36:02,987
I hope I've got your fire
when I'm your age.

817
01:36:02,988 --> 01:36:06,324
You will not live beyond today.
That I promise you.

818
01:36:22,466 --> 01:36:26,052
Hyde. Come on.

819
01:36:26,053 --> 01:36:28,304
My formula's done.

820
01:36:48,576 --> 01:36:50,285
Watch out!

821
01:36:55,708 --> 01:36:58,918
Think you'll come in here
and wreck this? I'll rebuild it.

822
01:37:08,846 --> 01:37:11,598
There'll be others like me,
Quatermain.

823
01:37:11,599 --> 01:37:14,350
You can't kill the future.

824
01:38:10,991 --> 01:38:13,618
Do you ever get tired of being wrong?

825
01:38:13,619 --> 01:38:18,206
The League, me, Skinner.

826
01:38:18,207 --> 01:38:19,666
Wrong.

827
01:38:19,667 --> 01:38:22,877
Wrong about your little
American friend as well.

828
01:38:22,878 --> 01:38:25,588
Do you really think
he's ready for action?

829
01:38:25,589 --> 01:38:29,217
I think you've trained him about as well
as you trained your son.

830
01:39:04,211 --> 01:39:05,962
Get him.

831
01:39:11,635 --> 01:39:14,053
-It's too far.
-Take your time.

832
01:39:14,054 --> 01:39:16,180
You're ready.

833
01:39:22,146 --> 01:39:25,189
Take your time.

834
01:39:58,265 --> 01:39:59,891
I got him.

835
01:40:02,937 --> 01:40:07,774
May this new century be yours, son...

836
01:40:09,276 --> 01:40:12,153
...as the old one was mine.

837
01:41:01,286 --> 01:41:05,623
You remember he swore Africa
wouldn't let him die?

838
01:41:05,624 --> 01:41:08,042
I wish the old boy had been right.

839
01:41:12,339 --> 01:41:14,632
What's next?

840
01:41:14,633 --> 01:41:16,843
I have long hidden away
from the world.

841
01:41:16,844 --> 01:41:22,390
Now I wish to see it anew
as the century turns.

842
01:41:22,391 --> 01:41:25,309
You're all welcome to join me.

843
01:41:25,310 --> 01:41:28,438
We've all been hiding
in one form or another.

844
01:41:30,691 --> 01:41:33,317
The Nautilus awaits.

845
01:41:33,318 --> 01:41:35,570
So who's coming?

846
01:41:38,782 --> 01:41:41,659
So long, old chap.

847
01:41:41,660 --> 01:41:43,786
Goodbye, Allan.

848
01:41:50,586 --> 01:41:52,670
Goodbye.

849
01:41:55,591 --> 01:41:57,383
Thanks.

